<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josephinism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josephinism is the name given to the policies of Joseph II of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a> and the tradition they created. It subordinated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> to the state, dissolving hundreds of contemplative monasteries, granting toleration to Protestants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1781–1782 and training clergy in state seminaries. It also promoted a centralised, rational administration staffed by educated officials loyal to the state, a tradition that outlived Joseph.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephinism.jpg" alt="Josephinism" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton von Maron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_von_Maron_006.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Joseph II, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>, received a rigorous education shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>'s stress on rationality and order, and the tangled administration of Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> left him deeply dissatisfied. As junior co-regent from 1765 he pressed reforms that his mother and her chancellor Kaunitz largely directed: the suppression of 71 of the 467 monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, a minimum age of 24 for monks, the effective dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> order and an Urbarium law limiting peasants' feudal obligations in Bohemia. Josephinism as a political force thus predated Joseph, in a less radical form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>After 1780 Joseph issued decrees and Patents aimed at a unitary, centralised and largely secular state with fewer arbitrary feudal institutions. Two Patents of 1 November 1781 ended fines and corporal punishment of Bohemian serfs and lords' control over their marriage, movement and occupation, though nobles applied them inconsistently. An edict of February 1781 limited censorship to blasphemy, subversion and immorality, and his censors banned about 900 tracts a year, against 4,000 before.</p><p>Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> could no longer write directly to the Curia, and 60 million florins from the dissolved monasteries paid for 1,700 new parishes and welfare institutions. Six state General Seminaries trained priests, and a Marriage Patent of 1783 treated marriage as a civil contract. Joseph lifted many restrictions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1782 but still held that they had repellent characteristics, and his decrees did not cover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1784 Joseph brought the crowns of St. Stephen and St. Wenceslas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> with German in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s administration. Resistance from clergy and nobles grew, and the reforms applied to the Austrian Netherlands in 1787 contributed greatly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a>. Before his death in 1790 he had to rescind many administrative reforms and return the Hungarian crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> reversed some of them but held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands together, while historians have remembered Joseph as an enlightened ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josephinism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Theresian and Josephine reforms became a model of enlightened absolutism, and the bureaucratic tradition of <b>Josephinism</b>, with its educated, state-minded civil service, outlived its founder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
